    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. To follow up, I'm specifically worried about the \"atypical\" classification being flagged by payers. If a clinician notes something like \"irregular, dark, asymmetric patches with a rough, almost scaly texture\" that looks much more chaotic than a typical nevus, but we can't definitively label it as a malignancy yet, how do we navigate the L-series (diseases of the skin) vs. the D-series (neoplasms) hierarchy to ensure we aren't over-coding or under-coding based purely on the visual irregularity alone?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For atypical lesions, it's essential to capture the nuances of the lesion's appearance while avoiding over-coding. You can use the L-series codes (skin diseases) and consider the clinical significance of the findings. Provide context, such as the location, size, and any other relevant characteristics. If you can't determine malignancy yet, you can use L85.67 (changes in skin pigmentation or texture) or, more specifically L85.32 (unspecified, other, skin and subcutaneous tissue disorders), to acknowledge the atypical nature without assuming malignancy.",
      "source": "target",
