    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm seeing a lot of cases where the morphology is just... unsettling. Like, extremely jagged, almost \"grotesque\" borders that seem to spread in an unpredictable, creeping pattern, or skin that looks pitted and uneven in a way that feels fundamentally different from typical pathology. When the clinical notes use descriptors that imply a more \"erratic\" or \"gnarly\" appearance, does that change how we should prioritize the hierarchy, or should we strictly stick to the standard L-series codes regardless of how much the morphology deviates from the norm?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
